course: dictionary
lesson: 2437
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A dagger is a short knife with a sharp, pointed blade, made for stabbing. You can also meet dagger in other ways, so read the words around it to find out which meaning is right.
explain: «The kingfisher dives head-first from a perch and its dagger beak enters the water with little splash.» A kingfisher does not carry a knife, so dagger here must mean long, sharp and pointed, like a dagger. Ask: what would fit here? A sharp point fits the sentence.
explain: «The cassowary has dagger-like claws» tells you the claws are shaped like a dagger: long, sharp and pointed. The word -like means "similar to", so dagger-like means "a bit like a dagger".
explain: Some phrases use dagger in a special way. «Look daggers» means to give someone a very angry look, not to throw a knife. «Cloak-and-dagger» means secret and spy-like. When a phrase seems strange, check whether the words around it make sense with the usual meaning.
fact: «Dagger» means a short knife with a sharp, pointed blade, made as a weapon.
fact: In books, a dagger (†) is a small mark that points to a note, or sits next to a name to show that the person has died.
fact: «Look daggers» means to give someone a very angry look.
fact: «Cloak-and-dagger» means secret and spy-like, as in a spy story.
fact: In the UK, Ireland and Australia it is against the law to carry a knife like a dagger in public.
exercise: «The cassowary has a dagger-like claw up to about 12 centimetres on each foot.» What does dagger-like mean? | Heavy and very slow | Soft and round like a ball | Blue with feathers all over | *Long, sharp and pointed, like a dagger
exercise: Dad says, «She looked daggers at me when I took the last biscuit.» What does looked daggers mean? | *She gave me a very angry look | She was hiding a secret | She was carrying a knife | She dropped the biscuit
exercise: Which animal in these sentences hunts with long dagger teeth? | Northern Australian bird | *Goliath tigerfish | Cassowary | Kingfisher
test: What does «cloak-and-dagger» mean? | A type of fast train | *Secret and spy-like | A heavy coat for winter | Very loud and noisy
test: In a book, what does a dagger mark (†) usually do? | Show a word is rude | Show the page is torn | *Point to a note | Show the book is very old
exam: Which phrase means "ready to fight"? | *at daggers drawn | dagger-like | cloak-and-dagger | look daggers
